WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.520
I'm Rick Lifton, the president of the
Rockefeller University and I'm thrilled

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.299
to have the chance to welcome you to our
campus this morning. We're grateful that

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:20.890
so many of you have come from far and
wide to be part of this impressive

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:26.109
gathering. Based on the attendees in the
ambitious agenda for the meeting I'm

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:30.579
sure this is going to be an exciting two
days here at Rockefeller. We share your

00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:33.880
commitment and passion for increasing
the public's awareness and understanding

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.770
and appreciation for the scientific
method and for expanding awareness of

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.570
the vital roles that science plays in
our society. I trust that the network and

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.080
community that you'll be building here
through this terrific conference will

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.800
play an important role in your future
endeavors and we're honored to have you

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.240
here at the University for the next two
days. In an era in which the objective

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.620
reality of truth and facts and the
ability to appropriately weigh the

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.449
strength of evidence seems under siege
on a daily basis in our society I

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:09.130
believe no effort is more important than
educating the public about the methods

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.299
by which we have come to understand the
world around us and the benefits that

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:18.130
that knowledge has brought to our global
society. This rightfully begins in K

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:22.869
through 12 education, inspiring children
about the wonders of nature and the

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:26.469
methods by which we achieve new insights
into its workings.

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:31.090
This should continue throughout life.
This is why I'm particularly delighted

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.689
to welcome you all to campus today! I
moved here to Rockefeller from Yale in

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:41.829
2016 and I can tell you that one of the
many factors that impressed me here was

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:46.450
a university strong commitment to
engaging the public in science. We're

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.859
enormously proud of our scientific
culture here at Rockefeller and of our

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:56.109
efforts to spark interest, public
interest, in an awareness of the key role

00:01:56.109 --> 00:02:01.509
of science in advancing health and
societal goals. Just two nights ago we

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:06.579
had an overflow crowd of more than 400
lay people at Caspary auditorium just

00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:11.440
across the walkway for the presentation
of the Pearl Meister green guard award

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.000
the world's most prominent award for
women in biomedical science to the CRISPR

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.050
pioneer Jennifer Doudna that was
followed yesterday

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.400
morning by more than a hundred
and fifty participants in our women in

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.190
science program who attended Jennifer's
public science lecture at 8 a.m. here in

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.630
this room with an overflow crowd. Events
like these increase awareness of the

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:39.670
importance of society in our city and
around the world. We do a dozen events

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:44.350
like this for adults on campus each year
including our women in science, our

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:50.380
parents in science, and insight lecture
series. On a k-12 front we're immensely

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:55.870
proud of the RockEDU outreach program
led by Jeanne Garbarino that brings New

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:00.190
York City middle school students high
school students and stem teachers to

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.290
campus to engage in hands-on science
curricula with opportunities for deeper

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:09.730
lab experience and summer science
programs for students who catch the

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:15.250
science bug. Two other of my favorite
events of the year are our talking science

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.810
program that brings high school students
to campus to learn more about science

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:24.580
discovery from some of the world's great
scientists via interactive presentations

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.840
and hands-on demonstrations and our
science Saturday program which brings a

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.000
thousand area area middle schoolers and
their parents to campus for a remarkable

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.160
day of hands-on science with more than
30 engaging science stations directed by

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:43.600
working scientists from Rockefeller and
colleagues from our sister institutions

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.940
across the city. This mission also
extends to educating our government

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.160
officials about the importance of public
support for science as a public good.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:58.120
Early in my term here one of our
trustees, Bill Ford, and I had the

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.710
opportunity to organize a small group of
scientific leaders from academia and

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:07.600
industry to meet on two occasions with
the new Trump administration which had

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:13.060
early on proposed a 20% reduction in the
funding for biomedical research in order

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:17.140
to educate them about the vital impact
that public support for biomedical

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:21.549
science has had on the dramatic increase
in healthy lifespan over the last

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:26.500
century as well as on the nation's
economic vitality. It's been gratifying

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.100
that with the strong support for
Congress the NIH budget has in fact

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.400
increased 20% over the last three years.
Commitment and engagement are critical.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.220
Before I turn things over to Jeannie I
also want to take a moment just to tell

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.820
you a little bit about Rockefeller
University and give you some context for

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:48.370
where you are meeting today. Rockefeller
is a remarkable home for science. It was

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:54.250
founded in 1901 with its core mission
science for the benefit of humanity it's

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.300
dedicated to the research and graduate
education and biomedical and related

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:03.340
Natural Sciences. Over the last century
Rockefeller scientists have sought to

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.970
solve the deepest questions in
biomedical science with the knowledge

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:12.010
that that fundamental understanding of
life processes and disease are the

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:17.170
surest path to preventing diagnosing and
effectively treating human disease and

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:21.250
along the way they have made
transformative breakthroughs. For example

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:25.960
Avery's classic experiments with
pneumococcus demonstrating that DNA is

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.260
the chemical of heredity those
experiments were performed about a

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:33.330
hundred and fifty yards to the south
from here on the Rockefeller campus in

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:39.340
1944 and there are many other seminal
discoveries of that ilk. Although we only

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:44.230
have 80 faculty here on campus we're
home to some of the world's most amazing

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:50.290
scientists including 39 members of the
National Academy of scientists, 14 Howard

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:55.900
Hughes Medical Institute investigators, a
few weeks after my arrival here in 2016

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.640
Charlie Rice received the Lasker Award
for his discoveries about the hepatitis

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.200
C virus that led to the development of a
cure for this disease that affects 3

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.760
million Americans and a hundred and
seventy million people worldwide. At this

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.050
time last year Mike Young had just been
announced as the winner of the Nobel

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:19.660
Prize in medicine for his discovery of
the mechanisms of circadian rhythms that

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:24.190
link our sleep/wake cycle feeding and
behavior to the Earth's 24-hour

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:29.200
day/night cycle and just two weeks ago
David Alice was presented with the

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.600
Lasker Award for his discovery of the
role of histone modification in

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.850
eukaryotic gene regulation. An amazing 25
of the 250 professors in our history

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:45.130
have won the Nobel Prize in medicine or
chemistry. Our community of just over

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:49.240
2,000 people includes
students postdocs and administrative

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:53.979
